tools/ci - workflow files staged here because they cannot be pushed from
           a session whose token lacks `workflow' scope

	cp tools/ci/*.yml .github/workflows/
	git add .github/workflows && git commit

WHY THEY ARE NOT ALREADY THERE.  GitHub refuses a push that creates or
updates anything under .github/workflows unless the credential carries
the `workflow' scope:

	refusing to allow an OAuth App to create or update workflow
	`.github/workflows/ci.yml` without `workflow` scope

That is the same refusal that stopped 24 release tags from being pushed
on 11 October 2026 (see doc/open-issues-plan.ms, the CI/CD section).  So
these are ordinary files in tools/, which pushes fine, and moving them
is one command with a credential that has the scope.

WHAT IS HERE

  host-gates.yml    T0+T1.  Builds the toolchain and every kernel, then
                    runs tests/toolchain.  Minutes.  Every push.
  boardsuite.yml    T2.  The 29 QEMU configurations in the container,
                    with the patched qemu the Pi boards need.  Tens of
                    minutes.  Nightly and on demand.

AND ONE CHANGE THAT NEEDS NO NEW FILE, which is the most valuable single
edit and also cannot be made from here, because editing an existing
workflow needs the same scope:

	.github/workflows/quick-smoke.yml carries

		continue-on-error: true

	DELETE THAT LINE.  It makes the job a permanent green: it can
	fail outright and the check still reports success.

HOW FAR THESE HAVE BEEN VERIFIED, stated because it is not far.  The
build steps in host-gates.yml are transcribed from docker/build-and-run,
which is the recipe the container has been using, and the boardsuite
invocation is the one tools/boardsuite/run and docker/boardsuite.sh
document.  THE YAML ITSELF HAS NEVER RUN: this session has no Actions
runner, and the build chain could not be rehearsed locally either,
because `cd utils && mk install' overwrites MacOSX/arm64/bin -- the
toolchain binaries other sessions in this tree are using.  Expect the
first run to need a fix, and read the step names rather than trusting
the whole thing.

TWO THINGS THAT WILL BITE ON A RUNNER AND ARE HANDLED HERE

  - mkconfig carries an absolute ROOT and SYSHOST=MacOSX.  Both are
    rewritten in the build step, exactly as docker/build-and-run does.
    In this working tree that file is a load-bearing local edit and must
    not be committed; in CI it is a fresh clone and rewriting it is
    correct.

  - docker/boardsuite.sh defaults to BOARDSUITE_PLATFORM=linux/arm64.
    A GitHub x86_64 runner would emulate that and crawl, so
    boardsuite.yml sets linux/amd64 explicitly.

THE GRADING RULES these encode, each of which was paid for in
doc/open-issues-plan.ms:

  - judge a gate by its EXIT STATUS, never by grepping or tailing its
    output;
  - tests/toolchain has THREE states -- pass, defect, could not run --
    and a could-not-run is a self-disabled test, i.e. a silent coverage
    hole, not a yellow light;
  - on a boardsuite marker diff a REMOVED marker is a regression and an
    ADDED one means "re-record the baseline": both fail, differently,
    and neither is green;
  - keep the console rings as artifacts on success as well as failure,
    because a ring is this project's only post-mortem channel.

WHAT CI CANNOT DO HERE.  There is no board on a runner, so nothing about
four-processor behaviour, USB split transactions, framebuffer tearing, or
anything read with tools/jtag/* can be asserted at any tier.  A green CI
does not mean the board works.
